We can find a pattern for powers of $\mathbf{A}$ by computing a few powers:
\begin{align*}
\mathbf{A}^2 &= \mathbf{A} \mathbf{A} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 & 1 \\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 & 0 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 & 1 \\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 & 0 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\ 1 & 0 & 0 \end{pmatrix}, \\
\mathbf{A}^3 &= \mathbf{A} \mathbf{A}^2 = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 & 1 \\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 & 0 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\ 1 & 0 & 0 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix} = \mathbf{I},
\end{align*}where $\mathbf{I}$ is the identity matrix. We see that $\mathbf{A}^2$ is a cyclic permutation of the rows of $\mathbf{A},$ and $\mathbf{A}^3 = \mathbf{I}.$

Since $\mathbf{A}^3 = \mathbf{I},$ $\mathbf{A}^{100}$ is equal to $(\mathbf{A}^3)^{33} \mathbf{A},$ which is equal to $\mathbf{I}^{33} \mathbf{A} = \mathbf{A}.$ Therefore, $$\mathbf{A}^{100} = \boxed{\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 & 1 \\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 & 0 \end{pmatrix}}.$$The answer is: \begin{pmatrix}0&0&1\1&0&0\0&1&0\end{pmatrix}

