solution_steps:
We will use the Euclidean algorithm to find the greatest common divisor of $f(100)$ and $f(101)$.
First, we find the remainder when dividing $f(101)$ by $f(100)$:
\begin{align*}
f(101) &= (101)^2 - 101 + 2010 \\
&= 10201 - 101 + 2010 \\
&= 12210.
\end{align*}
Next, we find the remainder when dividing $f(100)$ by $12210$:
\begin{align*}
f(100) &= (100)^2 - 100 + 2010 \\
&= 10000 - 100 + 2010 \\
&= 1210.
\end{align*}
Continuing with the remainder $1210$, we find the remainder when dividing $12210$ by $1210$:
\begin{align*}
12210 &= 10(1210) + 100.
\end{align*}
Finally, we find the remainder when dividing $1210$ by $100$:
\begin{align*}
1210 &= 12(100) + 10.
\end{align*}
Since the remainder is $10$, we have that the greatest common divisor of $f(100)$ and $f(101)$ is $\boxed{10}$.
The answer is: 10

